#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# function_analytic

#% text
# `function_analytic(f, x)` gives `true` when `f` is analytic at every real `x`, `false` otherwise; `function_analytic([f, cond], x)` on the part where `cond` holds.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Functions with singularities (poles, branch points of roots and logarithms) are not analytic.

#% item
# Absolute values, floors and other functions that are only piecewise analytic leave the call unevaluated.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# Sine and tangent:

#% cell
function_analytic(sin(x), x)
#% output
#= true

#% cell
function_analytic(tan(x), x)
#% output
#= false

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# On an interval without the poles:

#% cell
function_analytic([tan(x), -pi/2 < x and x < pi/2], x)
#% output
#= true

#% text
# Over the complexes:

#% cell
function_analytic(exp(x) + x, x, complexes)
#% output
#= true

#% cell
function_analytic(sqrt(x), x, complexes)
#% output
#= false

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `function_singularities` gives the singular points:

#% cell
function_singularities(tan(x), x)
#% output
#= cos(x) == 0

#% text
# Special functions that are entire, and the facts of the `assumptions:` option:

#% cell
[function_analytic(fresnel_c(x), x), function_analytic(log(x), x, assumptions: x > 0)]
#% output
#= [true, true]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `function_singularities`, `function_domain`
